An object of class \code{"lta_model"} with components including \code{delta},
\code{tau} (a list of transition matrices), \code{prevalences} (status prevalence by
occasion), \code{gamma} (posterior status probabilities by occasion), \code{mm},
\code{metrics} and \code{n_params}.

Two diagnostics come with it. \code{boundary} lists the transition cells the data
have driven to zero, and \code{smoothing_influence} gives, for each origin row,
the cases expected in it and the share of the estimate the \code{smoothing}
prior is carrying. \code{smoothing_influence} is \code{NULL} when \code{smoothing} is 0 or
when covariates predict the transitions, since the prior does not reach
them then.

With \code{n_classes} > 1 those parameters gain a class index: \code{delta} becomes a
classes-by-statuses matrix, \code{tau} a list of per-class lists, and
\code{class_weights}, \code{class_posterior} and \code{gamma_by_class} are added.
\code{prevalences} stays the whole-sample marginal, with the per-class ones in
its \code{"by_class"} attribute; \code{\link[=status_prevalences]{status_prevalences()}} and
\code{\link[=transition_matrix]{transition_matrix()}} take a \code{class} argument to reach them. Standard
errors are not available for a mixture over chains and \code{se} is \code{NULL}.

\code{metrics$entropy} (the headline number, printed by \code{print()}) is the
relative entropy of the joint latent-status path across all occasions at
once. This matches the single LTA entropy figure reported by one widely
used program. The other reports an entropy per latent variable instead;
the occasion-weighted average of that quantity, on this package's
normalising convention, is \code{metrics$entropy_by_occasion} (printed by
\code{summary()}). The two programs' own entropy figures use different
normalising conventions for the per-latent-variable quantity, so to
compare \code{entropy_by_occasion} against the other program's figures,
convert with \code{1 - (1 - r2) * log(K) / H(pi_hat)}, where \code{r2} is the
package's value, \code{K} is \code{n_statuses}, and \code{H(pi_hat)} is the entropy of
that occasion's estimated status proportions (from
\code{\link[=status_prevalences]{status_prevalences()}}).
}
\description{
Fits a latent transition model: each person occupies a latent \emph{status} at each
occasion, measured by the same indicators every time, and may move between
statuses from one occasion to the next. Where \code{\link[=fit_rmlca]{fit_rmlca()}} assigns a person
one class for the whole study, \code{fit_lta()} estimates both the prevalence of
each status and the \emph{incidence of change} between them (Collins & Lanza, 2010,
ch. 7).

Three things are estimated:
\describe{
\item{status prevalences}{how common each status is at the first occasion;}
\item{transition probabilities}{the chance of moving from each status to
each other status, as a square table read from row (earlier occasion) to
column (later occasion). There is one table per pair of adjacent
occasions unless \code{transition_invariance = "full"};}
\item{item parameters}{what people in each status tend to answer, which is
what gives the statuses their meaning.}
}

Cases with individual items or whole occasions missing are kept in the
analysis rather than dropped.

\strong{Why measurement invariance defaults to \code{"full"}.} If a status is not
defined identically at every occasion, an apparent "transition" mixes real
change in the person with a change in what the status means, and the two
cannot be told apart. Holding the item parameters equal across occasions
removes that ambiguity, and is the usual practice (Collins & Lanza,
sec. 7.11). It is a testable restriction: fit the model both ways and compare
them with \code{\link[=lr_test]{lr_test()}}.
}
